## What happened

In **.NET 11**, Apple's DSA support was removed from the macOS cryptography layer. `DSA.Create()` (and all DSA signing/verification) now **throws on macOS**. It still works on Windows and Linux.

## How to fix it

Since you only need to **verify** legacy DSA signatures, you have two options:

### Option 1 — Replace DSA with a supported algorithm (preferred long-term)

Re-sign existing artifacts with **ECDSA** or **RSA**, then update verification code:

```csharp
// Before (breaks on macOS in .NET 11)
using var dsa = DSA.Create();
dsa.ImportParameters(legacyParams);
bool ok = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);

// After
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create();
ecdsa.ImportParameters(newParams);
bool ok = ecdsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

### Option 2 — Platform-gate the DSA path (if you can't re-sign)

If you must keep verifying existing DSA signatures, run that code only on Windows/Linux:

```csharp
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    throw new PlatformNotSupportedException("DSA verification is not supported on macOS with .NET 11+.");

using var dsa = DSA.Create();
// ...
```

### Recommendation

Migrate away from DSA entirely — it is a legacy algorithm across all platforms. ECDSA is the modern drop-in replacement for digital signatures.
